{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqe1cey1lux","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Eager judge dispatch on node add via overlay eagerJudge signal","summary":"Judge C makes adjudication eager (event triggered on node add), not a standing periodic drain. The daemon stays dumb: it only SIGNALS which nodes have fresh unjudged candidate edges; the orchestration layer dispatches. New overlay field eagerJudge (node key to epoch stamp), persisted. overlay.markEagerJudge / clearEagerJudge are pure mutations; judge.eagerJudgeNodes returns marked nodes FIFO and self prunes any whose candidate edge set is fully judged (so a node never re dispatches once drained). judge.buildQueueForNode returns a node whole unverified context edge set (one dispatch covers all, never per edge). Seed callsites mark eager: a new task in routes overlay status when autowireNewTaskWholeGraph seeds candidates, and a new note in overlay note. In daemon decideOne, eagerJudgeDirective is the PRIMARY judge trigger computed BEFORE the periodic judgeDirective; it emits action judge_eager with nodes array (or an eager field on a spawn decision), concurrency capped at judgeParallelCap and headroom, with the excess staying marked for later ticks. The periodic depth driven judge_edges is now only a FALLBACK when no eager nodes pend (task D adds timeout fallback semantics). judge next gains a node query param that serves that node whole edge set in one slice and clears its eager mark on dispatch. orch loop SKILL documents the new judge_eager action plus eager field. Daemon restart required to go live (gated, not restarted).","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-14T17:05:36.169Z","vec":[-0.06721284240484238,0.013840601779520512,-0.0012373701902106404,0.020329417660832405,0.021204505115747452,-0.06026435270905495,0.022513289004564285,0.0041278814896941185,0.0025688204914331436,0.04126681759953499,-0.013114441186189651,-0.019005510956048965,0.01165097113698721,-0.09767553210258484,-0.0203177258372